config X58_PRO_E_BRINGUP_STAGE
	int "Experimental bring-up stage"
	range 0 11
	default 0
	help
	  Stage 0 proves reset, cache-as-RAM, and C entry only.
	  Stage 1 additionally identifies the ICH10R LPC function using a
	  read-only PCI configuration-space transaction.
	  Stage 2 programs only the narrow LPC decodes for Fintek configuration
	  port 0x4e and COM1 at 0x3f8, verifies the Super-I/O identity, enables
	  UART logical device 1, and transmits a bounded diagnostic banner.
	  Stage 3 retains all stage-2 checks, then enters the separate XIP
	  romstage from CBFS and halts there while still executing from CAR.
	  Stage 4 additionally runs the existing ICH10 early BAR setup for
	  RCBA, PMBASE, and GPIOBASE, then enables only the SMBus controller
	  I/O BAR.  The proven narrow LPC/UART path remains unchanged and every
	  programmed register is read back before the terminal POST code.
	  Stage 5 additionally performs exactly one bounded SMBus byte-data read
	  of DDR3 SPD type byte 2 at address 0x50.  It does not scan, retry,
	  train memory, touch X58/QPI/IMC state, or continue beyond CAR.
	  Stage 6 is the diagnostic follow-up: it records SMBus pin state, writes
	  only the documented SMBCLK release bit, verifies both lines idle, and
	  probes type byte 2 once at each standard SPD address 0x50 through 0x57.
	  It treats an exact DEV_ERR as no usable response and continues, but never
	  retries, dumps SPD, trains memory, or touches X58/QPI/IMC state.
	  Stage 7 requires the stage-6 result at address 0x54, then reads the
	  128-byte DDR3 SPD base block there with bounded byte-data commands and
	  verifies its revision-dependent JEDEC CRC.  It neither retries nor reads
	  the upper SPD block, and still does not touch X58/QPI/IMC or DRAM.
	  Stage 8 requires a valid stage-7 base block whose header declares 176
	  used bytes in a 256-byte DDR3 EEPROM.  It reads bytes 0x80 through 0xaf
	  twice, requires both copies to match, then uses coreboot's DDR3 decoder
	  to derive but not program one conservative DDR3-800 policy.  XMP bytes,
	  X58/QPI/IMC registers, DRAM, and payload execution remain untouched.
	  Stage 9 is the fail-closed header telemetry after a stage-8 rejection:
	  it repeats only the stage-7 base-block/CRC path, emits progress code
	  0x7a, and halts with 0x80 | (SPD byte 0 & 0x7f).  This reports the
	  used/total-size fields without another SMBus transaction.  Bit 7 is
	  omitted from the POST encoding because it only selects the base-CRC
	  coverage that has already passed; UART still reports the complete byte.
	  Stage 10 accepts only the now-observed 256-used/256-total header.  It
	  reads bytes 0x80 through 0xff twice, requires exact agreement, then uses
	  coreboot's DDR3 decoder and derives but does not program a conservative
	  DDR3-800 policy.  It performs no IMC, QPI, X58, or DRAM write.
	  Stage 11 enters an interactive UART ROM monitor after the same bounded
	  CPU, ICH10R, and SMBus setup.  It supports CPUID, port-I/O, PCI config,
	  linear/MMIO, MSR, and the stage-10 SPD/decode transaction.  Human writes
	  require a separate one-shot unlock command.  A permanent SerialICE-v1.5
	  stream mode provides emulator-facing accesses with immediate writes.  The
	  B06L revision additionally provides bounded X58 PHY scan-chain
	  accesses and opt-in channel-2/rank-0 RD and RCVEN experiment profiles.
	  Its exact RD path repeats the statically recovered MRS2/MRS3/MRS1/MRS0
	  and per-rank ZQCL sequence before a direct RD command, and can sweep all
	  recovered coarse values with bounded polls; none runs automatically.
	  With X58_PRO_E_B06M_BASEINIT selected, the opt-in baseinit command
	  reproduces the already live-proven one-channel base state before those
	  profiles.  B06N adds measured UART-TEMT MRS and phase gaps.  Both remain
	  locked and never execute at boot.
	  With X58_PRO_E_B06V0_VENDOR_INIT selected, separate VENDOR arming gates
	  expose local-blob verification, PCIEXBAR preparation, CSI and MINIT
	  research calls, guarded workspace inspection, and policy editing.  The
	  vendor path remains manual and terminal inside CAR.
	  With X58_PRO_E_B06V1_REG_SCRIPT selected, the same monitor additionally
	  accepts digest-bound register tables with bounded execution tracing and
	  optional best-effort rollback.  Script execution uses a distinct arm and
	  permanently closes the vendor-call path until a cold reset.
	  Invalid reads or writes can still hang or reset the machine; this stage
	  is a recovery-lab tool only.

